## 1. Framing: what "quiet-by-design" actually means as a technology stack

The quiet-airport conversation is dominated by branding — "silent airport," "peaceful terminal," "sensory-friendly." Underneath the marketing sits a specific and increasingly buildable technology stack. This brief taxonomizes that stack, names the vendors and airports that have deployed it, distinguishes what is off-the-shelf in 2026 from what still requires custom engineering, and separates independent operating evidence from vendor claims and press-release aspirations.

A defensible taxonomy has five layers. All five must be specified together; treating any one in isolation is the failure mode that produces branded "silent airports" that still generate constant gate calls or, worse, that meet the branding target and quietly fail life-safety intelligibility requirements.

1. **Passive acoustic envelope.** Ceiling and wall absorption, floor materials, glazing, and volumetric geometry that set the ambient sound floor and reverberation time (RT60). This is a building-design decision, not a technology procurement, and it is the layer most likely to be foreclosed if the standard arrives after schematic design.
2. **Zoned voice and mass-notification systems.** IP-based public address / voice alarm (PA/VA) systems that can address individual acoustically distinguishable spaces (ADS) rather than the whole terminal, with intelligibility validated to NFPA 72 thresholds.
3. **Automated and localized announcement generation.** Text-to-speech and phoneme-based systems (AviaVox, Simpleway, Inform) that replace human gate calls with short, multilingual, gate-only messages triggered by boarding events rather than by a gate agent's microphone.
4. **Redundant visual and digital information channels.** Flight information display systems (FIDS) sized for gate-side reading distance, mobile push (airline and airport apps), open messaging channels (SMS, Messenger, WhatsApp), and hearing-loop / Auracast / real-time-captioning services for deaf and hard-of-hearing travelers.
5. **Low-stimulation spaces and sensory-friendly infrastructure.** Dedicated quiet rooms, sensory rooms, hidden-disabilities programs (Sunflower lanyard), and the operational protocols that keep them staffed and functional.

A quiet-by-design standard that does not name a requirement in each layer will be gamed by whichever contractor holds the pen in the next design package.

