## 3. Evidence by layer

### 3.1 Passive acoustic envelope

Independent guidance converges on RT60 targets near **1.5 seconds** for a large terminal volume if speech is to be intelligible; specialized rooms (control towers, quiet rooms) target ~0.5 s. Practitioner guidance recommends broadband absorption on **25–40% of total wall and ceiling area** using materials of NRC ≥ 0.85 to move a hard-surfaced terminal into that range. Ceilings should be treated first because they are usually the largest unbroken hard surface. [Source: https://commercial-acoustics.com/guides/reverberation-time-for-different-rooms/] [Source: https://commercial-acoustics.com/sound-advice/how-much-sound-absorption-do-you-need/]

**MWAA implication.** IAD Concourse E (~435,000 sq ft, opens autumn 2026) is the immediate test. Once ceilings, floors, and glazing are locked, the ambient sound floor and RT60 are effectively set for the life of the concourse. Retrofit is possible but far more expensive than specification at design. For the Saarinen main terminal and Pelli's DCA National Hall, historic-preservation constraints add cost and lengthen the review path for absorptive interventions.

### 3.2 Zoned PA/VA systems

Munich Airport's PRAESENSA deployment — up to 3,000 system components and 10,000 audio zones on a single centrally managed network — is the current benchmark for what "gate-only paging" can be operationalized against. Warsaw Chopin's 2024 upgrade demonstrated the retrofit path: the digital head-end was placed onto existing IP and cabling, with flight-number/airline/destination announcements auto-generated from AODB events. [Source: https://www.boschbuildingtechnologies.com/xc/en/stories/munich-airport-relies-on-a-networked-public-address-and-voice-alarm-solution/] [Source: https://www.boschsecurity.com/us/en/news/customer-stories/airport-chopin-warsaw/]

**Off-the-shelf in 2026:** Bosch PRAESENSA and comparable IP-VA platforms (Zenitel, Atlas IED, Biamp) are commodity. **Still custom:** the acoustically distinguishable space (ADS) partition — the design decision about how the terminal is carved into zones — is bespoke to each floor plan and remains an acoustical-consultant deliverable.

### 3.3 Automated announcement generation

AviaVox is deployed at Schiphol, Heathrow, and airports handling under 100,000 passengers per year, with more than 35 languages supported. Simpleway and Inform Software are named in the 2024 GlobeNewswire market report as principal competitors. Vendor materials describe the value proposition as "preventing announcement deafness as part of a 'silent airport' policy" — a claim I flag as vendor-sourced. [Source: https://aviavox.com/about-us/our-customers/] [Source: https://www.globenewswire.com/news-release/2024/08/29/2937634/28124/en/Airport-Notification-Systems-Market-Report-2024-2030-with-Profiles-of-Inform-Software-International-Display-Systems-RESA-Siemens-Simpleway-Europe-SITA-Ultra-and-Electronics-Airport-Systems.html]

**Independent evidence gap.** I could not locate a peer-reviewed or audited before/after study of announcement volume, missed-boarding rate, or passenger satisfaction attributable to an AviaVox-style automation deployment. Vendor case studies do not qualify.

### 3.4 Redundant visual and digital channels

- **FIDS density.** No US federal standard prescribes screens-per-gate. London City's silent policy explicitly rests on FIDS "placed throughout the lounge and in restaurants" as the compensating channel. [Source: https://www.londoncityairport.com/at-the-airport/need-to-know/airport-policies/silent-policy]
- **Open messaging channels.** London City publishes real-time flight information via Facebook Messenger and Twitter/X — a low-cost redundancy that does not require passengers to install an airport app. [Source: https://www.holidayextras.com/airport-parking/london-city/on-airport/silent-terminal.html]
- **App adoption reality.** SITA's Passenger IT Insights track a steady mobile shift — 79% of passengers said in 2025 they were ready for a phone-based digital travel wallet, and Millennials/Gen Z booked ~30% of trips through airline apps versus ~15% for Boomers — but the same data confirm the generational and language-based gaps that a "check your app" default would concentrate on. [Source: https://aerospaceglobalnews.com/news/sita-2025-passenger-digital-trends/]
- **Accessibility channels.** Frankfurt's 2025 Auracast trial pushes gate calls directly to Bluetooth hearing aids and earbuds; MSP's live speech-to-text turns audio gate calls into on-monitor captions; SEA offers on-demand ASL VRI via smartphone. [Source: https://aerospaceglobalnews.com/news/frankfurt-airport-bluetooth-auracast-trial-accessibility/] [Source: https://metroairports.org/news/msp-airport-debuts-enhanced-accessibility-programs-travelers-who-are-deaf-hard-hearing] [Source: https://www.portseattle.org/blog/sea-introduces-new-tool-support-travelers-who-are-deaf-and-hard-hearing]

### 3.5 Low-stimulation spaces

- **ATL** opened the first US airport sensory room in 2016 on Concourse F; operates 6 a.m.–10 p.m. daily; runs Hidden Disabilities Sunflower Lanyard program. [Source: https://outcoast.com/sensory-friendly-u-s-airports-that-reduce-overstimulation-for-the-neurodivergent-traveler/]
- **PIT** operates Presley's Place — 1,500 sq ft on Concourse A, purpose-built with a mock cabin, jetway, and multiple quiet rooms; Sunflower lanyards distributed free at information desks. [Source: https://flypittsburgh.com/pittsburgh-international-airport/accessibility/sunflower-program/]
- **HEL** opened a 24-hour multi-faith Quiet Room at Gate 32 in the Schengen area in late 2025, mirroring a 2023 non-Schengen room that Finavia describes as one of its most-requested amenities. [Source: https://www.finavia.fi/en/newsroom/2025/peace-and-relaxation-before-flight-new-quiet-room-has-been-opened-helsinki-airports]
- **CPH** operates sound-dampened areas near Gates A10 and C15, a silent lounge near Gate A4, and quiet workspaces on Level 3 of Terminal 3. [Source: https://www.cph.dk/en/practical/seating-areas]

