## 4. Peer deployments and what they actually did

- **London City (LCY):** Silent terminal since **2008**. Announcements limited to emergencies and irregular operations. Compensating channels: FIDS in lounges and restaurants, live updates on Facebook Messenger and Twitter/X. Post-implementation customer surveys are described as favorable but the underlying passenger-satisfaction data is operator-reported, not independently audited. [Source: https://www.londoncityairport.com/at-the-airport/need-to-know/airport-policies/silent-policy] [Source: https://www.holidayextras.com/airport-parking/london-city/on-airport/silent-terminal.html]
- **Schiphol (AMS):** Describes itself as a silent airport with "virtually no announcements"; flight information, gate changes, delays, and cancellations shown on FIDS, on schiphol.nl, and in the Schiphol app. AviaVox is the announcement engine for the residual and emergency calls. Independent measurement of ambient level change is not published. [Source: https://www.schiphol.nl/en/assistance/flying-for-the-deaf-and-blind/]
- **Copenhagen (CPH):** "Silent airport" policy — voice announcements minimized "to avoid creating unnecessary stress." [Source: https://www.cph.dk/en/practical/seating-areas]
- **Helsinki-Vantaa (HEL):** Finavia moved to a quiet-airport model with no routine flight/passenger announcements in the general terminal; gate-area and emergency announcements retained. Multi-faith 24-hour quiet room added 2023 (non-Schengen) and 2025 (Schengen, Gate 32). [Source: https://www.finavia.fi/en/newsroom/2025/peace-and-relaxation-before-flight-new-quiet-room-has-been-opened-helsinki-airports]
- **Dubai (DXB):** Silent-airport program announced **June 2010**; no overhead terminal announcements; the airport now integrates biometric "smart tunnels" and Terminal 3's passport-free "red carpet corridor" — reducing the number of touchpoints that would otherwise generate audible interactions. [Source: https://media.dubaiairports.ae/dubai-international-gives-passengers-the-silent-treatment/]
- **Munich (MUC):** IP PA/VA replacement at scale — the reference point for what a modern zoned-paging platform can be built to control. [Source: https://www.boschbuildingtechnologies.com/xc/en/stories/munich-airport-relies-on-a-networked-public-address-and-voice-alarm-solution/]

**Two structural caveats before transferring any of these to IAD or DCA.** First, all five European peers operate under an airport-of-use framework in which the operator sets tenant announcement rules unilaterally; MWAA enforces those rules through the U&L, tenant agreements, and Design Manual, which lengthens the change path. Second, none of the named peers is subject to US TSA required-security-announcement guidance or FAA Part 139; regulatory transfer is not automatic.

