## 6. Honest assessment: real, hype, and what still requires custom work

**Real (off-the-shelf in 2026):**
- IP-native PA/VA with per-gate zoning (Bosch PRAESENSA, Zenitel, Biamp, Atlas IED).
- Multilingual synthetic-voice engines integrated with AODB/FIDS (AviaVox, Simpleway, Inform).
- STIPA measurement to validate NFPA 72 STI ≥ 0.50 during commissioning.
- Live speech-to-text captioning on gate monitors; on-demand ASL VRI; Sunflower lanyard programs; sensory rooms.
- FIDS and mobile-notification integration through AODB.

**Emerging but not yet standard:**
- Auracast direct-to-hearing-aid gate broadcast (Frankfurt trial 2025).
- Directional / parametric-array loudspeakers as gate-area point sources — commercially available but with thin published airport measurement evidence.

**Hype to discount:**
- The word "silent." No operating US Part 139 airport can be truly silent; NFPA 72 alone requires audible emergency messages at defined intelligibility. Marketing that implies otherwise is misleading and, if written into a standard, will collide with life-safety code.
- Vendor case studies claiming quantified passenger-satisfaction or missed-boarding improvements without publishing methodology.

**Still requires custom work:**
- The acoustically distinguishable space (ADS) map for each terminal.
- The announcement-governance policy — which categories of message (paging, security, boarding, irregular ops, emergency) are automated, which require human intervention, which are pushed to which channels — must be written to fit each airport's carrier and TSA operational reality.
- Historic-preservation acoustic interventions inside Saarinen's IAD main terminal and Goodman's DCA Terminal 1 require bespoke design.

